WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.238
If you pull a long rope at one spot and let go, the bump splits in two and runs both ways.

2
00:00:07.238 --> 00:00:10.213
Today we explain this with a formula.

3
00:00:10.213 --> 00:00:21.230
After the lesson you will solve the Cauchy problem for an infinite string with d’Alembert’s formula and check the answer by substitution.

4
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:26.306
The problem: u t t equals a squared u x x on the whole line.

5
00:00:26.306 --> 00:00:34.511
The initial shape u at x and zero is phi of x, and the initial velocity u t at x and zero is psi of x.

6
00:00:34.511 --> 00:00:41.106
From the last topic the general solution is f of x minus a t plus g of x plus a t.

7
00:00:41.106 --> 00:00:44.726
The two initial conditions determine f and g.

8
00:00:44.976 --> 00:00:53.730
The result is d’Alembert’s formula: u equals the average of phi at x minus a t and phi at x plus a t,

9
00:00:53.730 --> 00:01:00.403
plus one over two a times the integral of psi from x minus a t to x plus a t.

10
00:01:00.403 --> 00:01:04.910
Careful: the factor is one over two a, not one half.

11
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:12.628
Example: u t t equals nine u x x, initial shape x squared, initial velocity x.

12
00:01:12.628 --> 00:01:14.542
Here a equals three.

13
00:01:14.542 --> 00:01:22.584
The first part is the average of x minus three t squared and x plus three t squared,

14
00:01:22.584 --> 00:01:26.318
which is x squared plus nine t squared.

15
00:01:26.568 --> 00:01:34.195
Second part: one sixth times the integral of s from x minus three t to x plus three t.

16
00:01:34.195 --> 00:01:42.088
That is one twelfth of a difference of squares, twelve x t over twelve, which equals x t.

17
00:01:42.088 --> 00:01:47.054
Answer: u equals x squared plus nine t squared plus x t.

18
00:01:47.304 --> 00:01:47.911
Check.

19
00:01:47.911 --> 00:01:50.138
u t t equals eighteen.

20
00:01:50.138 --> 00:01:57.629
Nine times u x x is nine times two, which is eighteen: the equation holds.

21
00:01:57.629 --> 00:02:01.375
At t equals zero, u equals x squared.

22
00:02:01.375 --> 00:02:07.246
u t equals eighteen t plus x, which is x at t equals zero.

23
00:02:07.246 --> 00:02:09.878
All three conditions hold.

24
00:02:10.128 --> 00:02:17.550
The graph shows the initial pulse one over one plus x squared and its state at time three.

25
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:26.209
The pulse has split into two halves of half the height, which have reached x equals plus and minus three.

26
00:02:26.209 --> 00:02:30.662
The shape does not change, it only moves with speed a.

27
00:02:30.912 --> 00:02:37.235
Question: the initial shape is sine x, the velocity is zero, and a equals one.

28
00:02:37.235 --> 00:02:38.938
What is the solution?

29
00:02:38.938 --> 00:02:44.126
Try turning the sum of two travelling sine waves into a product.
